<p>The United Arab Emirates said it will leave OPEC and the wider OPEC+ alliance on May 1, 2026, ending nearly six decades in the producer group and removing one of its largest Gulf members from coordinated production targets. The announcement allows Abu Dhabi to set its own output policy after years of friction over quotas that capped production at about 3.2 million to 3.4 million barrels per day while national capacity has approached 4.85 million barrels per day and is targeted at 5 million barrels per day by 2027. The decision followed the Iran war and disruptions around the Strait of Hormuz, which sources said have constrained Gulf exports and limited any immediate ability by the UAE to raise shipments. Several analyses also linked the move to a widening rift with Saudi Arabia over oil policy and regional influence, while UAE officials described it as consistent with national economic and energy strategy.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ZHCDUT68WT9XrU918RphlrIn7SwQFHPVE-0U1_97L06tzVSQ1mXQLlZPSWl-S3lUb9uuhsQ5CyzvATCuuspcYyjttNMdiSpMupE5i53UzCbGwSw7Bkuc5PwgqtAJzvuQzs9IfdLmviJZZYVu2Q8WxzIc22DEYiYfDkKLuFjrlf3kfafvVErxIhMqqpG2UbRfDoneVZZ4Oie_rYZKbo-BLNQctQ5fPpFAfIPFcWbZ-_2yVxLLZCKslpMFWhcRuQqWklxfo_zF5lEvKocQmQ' alt='The UAE was pumping close to 30 per cent below what it was capable of.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Group leverage: OPEC accounts for roughly 40% of global oil output, but the UAE’s exit removes a producer that supplied about 3% of world crude before the US-Israeli war on Iran forced some Gulf curbs.</li><li>Alliance cohesion: Five OPEC+ sources said the move surprised the group, while delegates and analysts said the remaining alliance was still likely to coordinate oil supply policy.</li><li>Diplomatic review: A UAE official told Reuters that Abu Dhabi is reviewing the utility of multilateral memberships but is not considering further withdrawals at this time.</li></ul><blockquote>The UAE has invested heavily in expanding its production capacity and has been increasingly uncomfortable with output constraints, particularly amid uneven compliance across the group, - Carole Nakhle</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>UAE Energy Minister Suhail Mohamed al-Mazrouei: He told Reuters the withdrawal was driven by the need to meet rising global energy demand, while the same analysis said freedom to ramp up production without OPEC constraints was likely another motive. (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330457615/uae-exit-strips-opec-of-clout-risks-bitter-price-war'>Aaj News</a>)</li><li>Chinese expert cited by South China Morning Post: The expert said the UAE’s exit could bring “even bigger trouble” and reflected a widening fracture inside the Gulf Cooperation Council. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3351896/uaes-exit-opec-could-bring-even-bigger-trouble-chinese-expert'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Vandana Hari, Energy Connects: Hari said the oil-market question is how quickly supply can normalize once Strait of Hormuz flows resume. (<a href='https://www.energyconnects.com/opinion/thought-leadership/2026/april/uae-s-opec-exit-could-speed-up-post-hormuz-market-normalisation/'>Energy Connects</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/india/639272-uaes-opec-exit-natural-move/'>UAE’s OPEC exit a natural move – top Russian expert</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/27d986e5-a1d9-4467-8b1e-d96cec4cfb71'>UAE withdrawal from Opec reopens rift with Saudi Arabia</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/china/diplomacy/article/3351896/uaes-exit-opec-could-bring-even-bigger-trouble-chinese-expert'>The UAE’s exit from Opec could bring ‘even bigger trouble’: Chinese expert</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/29/uae-quits-opec'>UAE quits OPEC</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.spiked-online.com/2026/04/29/the-uaes-exit-from-opec-is-bigger-than-the-price-of-oil/'